<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 8 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Founders of World Religions</h1><p class="path-tagline">Eight teachers whose words outlived every empire of their day.</p><p class="path-intro">The longest-lived institutions in history are not states but faiths. This path visits eight founders and reformers in rough chronological order, treating each historically: what we know of their lives, what they taught, and how communities carried those teachings across continents and millennia.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster"><span class="path-step-ph">1</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1200–1000 BCE (disputed)</span><span class="path-step-name">Zoroaster</span><span class="path-step-why">The prophet of ancient Iran whose vision of truth against the lie is among the oldest revealed religions.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Gautama Buddha</span><span class="path-step-why">The prince who left a palace to understand suffering, and whose middle way crossed all Asia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/confucius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · 551–479 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Confucius</span><span class="path-step-why">A failed official whose teaching on duty and ritual became the operating system of East Asian states.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/laozi"><span class="path-step-ph">4</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · 6th–4th century BCE (traditional)</span><span class="path-step-name">Laozi</span><span class="path-step-why">The elusive sage of the Daodejing and the way that cannot be spoken.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 4 BCE – c. 30/33 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Jesus</span><span class="path-step-why">A teacher in Roman Judaea whose following became the faith of empires.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad"><span class="path-step-ph">6</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 570–632</span><span class="path-step-name">Muhammad</span><span class="path-step-why">The prophet of Islam, whose community became a civilisation within a century of his death.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak"><span class="path-step-ph">7</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1469–1539</span><span class="path-step-name">Guru Nanak</span><span class="path-step-why">The founder of Sikhism, teaching one God and the equality of all people.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1483–1546</span><span class="path-step-name">Martin Luther</span><span class="path-step-why">The monk whose ninety-five theses split Western Christendom and remade Europe's map.</span></span></a></li></ol>
